text:
Q: openssl sha256sum hash contains output file as part of input when using find command to list the files A: <p>As others have pointed out, the shell executes <code>&gt; &quot;$1/hashedoutput.sha256&quot;</code> to create the output file before it runs <code>find</code>. Given what you've told us so far there's really only these options:</p>
<ol>
<li><p>Do not save the output in the same directory as you're reading the input from:</p>
<pre><code>gen_sha256sum () {
    find &quot;$1&quot; -maxdepth 1 -type f -exec sha256sum {} + &gt; &quot;${1}_hashedoutput.sha256&quot;
}
</code></pre>
</li>
<li><p>Save the output to a temp file first and remember to remove the output file before reading the input next time (note the use of <code>(...)</code> instead of <code>{...}</code> to delimit the function body so we can use a cleanup <code>trap</code> on exiting the subshell/function without impacting the rest of your code):</p>
<pre><code>gen_sha256sum () (
    tmp=$(mktemp) &amp;&amp;
    trap 'rm -f &quot;$tmp&quot;; exit' EXIT &amp;&amp;
    rm -f &quot;$1/hashedoutput.sha256&quot; &amp;&amp;
    find &quot;$1&quot; -maxdepth 1 -type f -exec sha256sum {} + &gt; &quot;$tmp&quot; &amp;&amp;
    mv -- &quot;$tmp&quot; &quot;$1/hashedoutput.sha256&quot;
)
</code></pre>
</li>
<li><p>Exclude <code>hashedoutput.sha256</code> from the list of files to be operated on by <code>sha256sum</code>:</p>
<pre><code>gen_sha256sum () {
    find &quot;$1&quot; -maxdepth 1 ! -name 'hashedoutput.sha256' -type f -exec sha256sum {} + &gt; &quot;$1/hashedoutput.sha256&quot;
}
</code></pre>
</li>
</ol>
<p>Option &quot;1&quot; is obviously the most concise and robust (e.g. it'd work even if you had a file named <code>hashedoutput.sha256</code> within <code>$1</code> that you <em>did</em> want included in the <code>sha256sum</code> processing) so I'd go with that.</p>
<p>Having said that, if the files you want to get the <code>sha256sum</code> of had some common part of their file name such as an extension like <code>.txt</code> then you could tweak the <code>find</code> to only find those files so let's call that option &quot;4&quot; (which is really just a positive variation of the negative option &quot;3&quot; above) and then that would be a reasonable alternative to &quot;1&quot;:</p>
<pre><code>gen_sha256sum () {
    find &quot;$1&quot; -maxdepth 1 -name '*.txt' -type f -exec sha256sum {} + &gt; &quot;$1/hashedoutput.sha256&quot;
}
</code></pre>
